## 📌 SECTION 3: Agent-by-Agent Deep Dive

### 🤖 Agent 1: Helper Agent ([helper_agent.py](file:///e:/Heart_Stroke_Prediction/test_helper_agent.py))
| Aspect | Detail |
|--------|--------|
| **Purpose** | Extract structured medical JSON from plain English text |
| **LLM** | Ollama local — `llama3:8b` |
| **Why local?** | Zero API cost for a parsing task that doesn't need 70B intelligence |
| **Key technique** | Strict system prompt forces JSON-only output; code finds `{` and `}` to parse |
| **Edge case handling** | Returns `{"error": "Invalid JSON"}` if LLM outputs garbage |

**Follow-up Q: "What if the user types nonsense?"**
> "The [helper_agent_process()](file:///e:/Heart_Stroke_Prediction/agents/helper_agent.py#49-62) wraps the JSON parsing in a try-except. If the LLM returns non-JSON, the system returns an error dict and the UI shows it to the user. In production, I'd add an Input Validation Agent as a guardrail before this step."

---

### 🤖 Agent 2: Prediction Agent ([prediction_agent.py](file:///e:/Heart_Stroke_Prediction/test_prediction_agent.py))
| Aspect | Detail |
|--------|--------|
| **Purpose** | Run KNN inference on the 11 health features |
| **Model** | `KNeighborsClassifier` saved as [KNN_heart.pkl](file:///e:/Heart_Stroke_Prediction/KNN_heart.pkl) |
| **Preprocessing** | Manual one-hot encoding + MinMax scaling |
| **Key innovation** | 3 cleaning functions handle messy LLM outputs |

**The 3 cleaning functions (important to mention!):**
- [clean_fastingbs(value)](file:///e:/Heart_Stroke_Prediction/agents/prediction_agent.py#11-23) — Converts "Yes", "High", "Fine" → 0 or 1
- [clean_exercise_angina(value)](file:///e:/Heart_Stroke_Prediction/agents/prediction_agent.py#25-28) — Converts "YES", "1", "TRUE" → "Y" or "N"
- [clean_numeric(value, default=0)](file:///e:/Heart_Stroke_Prediction/agents/prediction_agent.py#30-36) — Converts "Fine", "Normal" → numeric safely

**Follow-up Q: "Why KNN and not XGBoost or Random Forest?"**
> "KNN was chosen as the foundational baseline because: (1) it's non-parametric and handles the small dataset well, (2) it's conceptually aligned with medicine — diagnosing by finding 'similar past patients', (3) the focus of this project was building the agentic pipeline, not squeezing 2% more accuracy. Swapping to XGBoost is a one-line change in production — `model = XGBClassifier()` — because my pipeline is model-agnostic."

**Follow-up Q: "What's the accuracy?"**
> "On this dataset, KNN achieved strong baseline performance. But more importantly, for a medical system, I optimized for **Recall (Sensitivity)** — catching all true positives — not just accuracy. A model that says 'No Heart Disease' every time gets 90% accuracy on an imbalanced dataset but kills patients."

---

### 🤖 Agent 3: Reasoning Agent ([reasoning_agent.py](file:///e:/Heart_Stroke_Prediction/test_reasoning_agent.py))
| Aspect | Detail |
|--------|--------|
| **Purpose** | Generate medical interpretation of the ML prediction |
| **LLM** | Groq API — `llama-3.3-70b-versatile` |
| **Why 70B?** | Medical reasoning requires deep contextual understanding of feature interactions |
| **Safety** | System prompt enforces: "NEVER give a medical diagnosis" |
| **Also has** | A [medical_chat()](file:///e:/Heart_Stroke_Prediction/agents/reasoning_agent.py#69-98) function for follow-up questions (uses same 70B model) |

**Follow-up Q: "What if the LLM hallucinates?"**
> "Three safeguards: (1) The LLM does NOT make the prediction — the KNN model does. The LLM only interprets. (2) The system prompt explicitly bans diagnoses and medication names. (3) In production, I'd add an Output Moderation Agent that scans for drug names or definitive diagnostic statements."

---

### 🤖 Agent 4: Lifestyle Agent ([lifestyle_agent.py](file:///e:/Heart_Stroke_Prediction/test_lifestyle_agent.py))
| Aspect | Detail |
|--------|--------|
| **Purpose** | Generate personalized health recommendations |
| **LLM** | Groq API — `llama-3.1-8b-instant` |
| **Why 8B (not 70B)?** | Smart cost optimization — lifestyle advice is structured and formulaic, doesn't need 70B depth |
| **Outputs** | 6 categories: diet, exercise, stress, sleep, foods to avoid, immediate steps |

**Follow-up Q: "Why use a different model size for different agents?"**
> "This is a production-grade cost optimization strategy. The Reasoning Agent needs to understand complex feature interactions (BP + Age + Cholesterol combined), so it needs 70B. The Lifestyle Agent generates structured lists — a task an 8B model handles perfectly. This reduces API costs by ~10x for that agent call while maintaining quality."

---

### 🤖 Agent 5: Report Agent ([report_agent.py](file:///e:/Heart_Stroke_Prediction/test_report_agent.py))
| Aspect | Detail |
|--------|--------|
| **Purpose** | Generate a professional PDF health report |
| **Library** | ReportLab (Python) |
| **Content** | User info, health features table, prediction, reasoning, lifestyle, disclaimer |
| **Design** | Custom header, double border, A4 format, tabular data display |

**Follow-up Q: "Why a PDF and not just screen output?"**
> "Two reasons: (1) Patients need a document they can physically take to their doctor. A screen is ephemeral. (2) In a real telehealth deployment, the PDF serves as a formal record. It contains a legal disclaimer and can be timestamped and archived."

