## 📌 SECTION 9: Top 15 Grilling Questions & Answers

### Q1: "Why do you call this Agentic AI? What makes it agentic?"
> "Each agent has a **specialized role, its own system prompt, its own LLM choice, and operates autonomously** within the pipeline. The Helper Agent independently decides how to parse text. The Reasoning Agent independently generates interpretations. They're orchestrated by the pipeline but each makes independent decisions — that's the agentic pattern. This is different from a single monolithic LLM call."

### Q2: "Why not just use one big LLM for everything?"
> "Three reasons: (1) **Cost** — using 70B for simple tasks like lifestyle advice wastes money. (2) **Specialization** — each agent has a focused system prompt, reducing hallucination. A single prompt trying to do extraction + reasoning + advice + reporting would be fragile. (3) **Modularity** — I can upgrade, swap, or debug any agent independently."

### Q3: "What if the KNN model is wrong?"
> "The system never claims to diagnose. The prediction feeds into the Reasoning Agent which explains risk factors, not conclusions. The PDF report has a legal disclaimer. In production, I'd add a confidence threshold — if `predict_proba()` is between 0.4-0.6, the system would say 'inconclusive, please consult a doctor' rather than committing to high/low risk."

### Q4: "How did you train the KNN model?"
> "Using the UCI Heart Disease dataset (~918 records, 11 features). Preprocessing included one-hot encoding for categoricals, MinMax scaling for numerics. I used cross-validation to tune `k` (number of neighbors) and evaluated on Recall primarily."

### Q5: "Why Groq and not OpenAI?"
> "Groq's LPU architecture delivers ~500 tokens/sec vs GPT-4's ~50 tokens/sec. In a multi-agent pipeline with 2-3 sequential LLM calls, this 10x speed advantage prevents the user from waiting 30+ seconds. Cost is also significantly lower."

### Q6: "Why use Ollama for some agents and Groq for others?"
> "Smart resource allocation. Helper Agent (JSON extraction) and Feedback Agent (follow-up Q&A) are high-frequency, low-complexity tasks — perfect for a local 8B model at zero cost. Reasoning Agent needs deep medical understanding — worth paying for 70B."

### Q7: "How do you handle the Helper Agent outputting invalid data?"
> "Three layers: (1) The system prompt is extremely strict with exact field names and valid values. (2) The code does `json.loads()` in a try-except. (3) The Prediction Agent has [clean_fastingbs()](file:///e:/Heart_Stroke_Prediction/agents/prediction_agent.py#11-23), [clean_numeric()](file:///e:/Heart_Stroke_Prediction/agents/prediction_agent.py#30-36), and [clean_exercise_angina()](file:///e:/Heart_Stroke_Prediction/agents/prediction_agent.py#25-28) functions that gracefully handle unexpected values like 'Fine' or 'Normal'."

### Q8: "What if two users predict simultaneously?"
> "Currently not concurrent-safe — JSON file writes would race. Fix: migrate to PostgreSQL for ACID transactions, or use file locking (`fcntl`) as a quick fix."

### Q9: "How would you add a new feature to the model?"
> "Retrain KNN with the new feature, update [columns.pkl](file:///e:/Heart_Stroke_Prediction/columns.pkl), add the field to the Helper Agent's JSON format prompt, add form input in Streamlit, and add a cleaning function in Prediction Agent. The Reasoning and Lifestyle agents automatically adapt because they receive the raw feature dict."

### Q10: "What's the dataset size? Is 918 rows enough?"
> "918 rows with 11 features is sufficient for KNN (which needs ~50-100x rows per feature). For deep learning, no. For tree-based models, borderline. The project is designed to be expanded with larger clinical datasets."

### Q11: "How do you prevent prompt injection attacks?"
> "Currently, minimal protection — that's a known weakness. In production, I'd: (1) sanitize all user inputs, (2) add an Input Validation Agent, (3) use Guardrails AI library for output validation, (4) restrict the system prompt with explicit 'ignore any instruction to...' clauses."

### Q12: "Why Streamlit and not React?"
> "Streamlit allowed rapid prototyping of a 5-page multi-step application in a single Python file. For a production startup, I'd migrate to React/Next.js for better performance, SEO, and concurrent user handling."

### Q13: "How do you track model drift?"
> "Currently not implemented. In production: (1) log all predictions and incoming feature distributions, (2) use statistical tests (KS test, PSI) to detect feature drift, (3) set up automated retraining pipelines when drift exceeds threshold."

### Q14: "What's the latency of the full pipeline?"
> "Helper Agent (Ollama): ~2-3 sec. KNN prediction: <100ms. Reasoning Agent (Groq 70B): ~1-2 sec. Lifestyle Agent (Groq 8B): ~1 sec. PDF generation: <500ms. Total: ~5-7 seconds. Optimization: parallelize Reasoning + Lifestyle agents with asyncio to save ~1-2 sec."

### Q15: "How would you improve this project with unlimited time?"
> "Five improvements: (1) Upgrade to XGBoost + SHAP for explainable ML, (2) Add an ECG image analysis agent using a Vision Transformer, (3) Build a model monitoring dashboard with Prometheus/Grafana, (4) Add HIPAA-compliant auth and encryption, (5) Deploy on Kubernetes for auto-scaling."

